  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nConditions are quiet and comfortable out there this afternoon. \nTemperatures range from the upper 70s to middle 80s with a nice \nbreeze out of the NNE. Dry air advection from high pressure \ndropping in from the north is clearing away a lake-enhanced low \nstratus deck and leaving behind sunny skies. The clear skies and\ndry air will allow early morning lows to drop into the upper \n50s to around 60 tomorrow, a little warmer in and around \nChicago. That high will be centered over the region tomorrow and\nThursday and keep rain chances away through the middle part of \nthe week. Highs are forecast in the lower and middle 80s \ntomorrow and Thursday.\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon showcases an apparent \nshortwave Pacific trough moving onshore over California. The \nwave will quietly lift northeast across the Rockies over the \nnext couple of days. By late Thursday, as the wave gets \nsandwiched between two centers of high pressure, it will quickly\nwind up into a closed midlevel low over the northern Plains \nbefore propagating southeast into the Midwest. The surface \nresponse will be a deepening center of low pressure progged to \nmove across IL late Friday into Saturday. A system of showers \nwill blossom around the developing low over the Plains on \nThursday and rain could move into our area from the west as \nearly as Friday morning. Chances build from west to east Friday \nafternoon and evening as coverage looks to overspread the area. \nWidespread showers are then expected Friday evening into \nSaturday morning. There's some uncertainty in what Saturday \nafternoon/evening will look like, but more scattered showers are\nfavored. Sometime Saturday-Sunday, the wave will become cutoff \nfrom the jet and wander around the lower Midwest into early next\nweek. While it's favored to stall over the Tennessee Valley and\nkeep the recurring rain chances south of our area early next \nweek, periodic showers may continue into portions of our CWA, \nespecially southern CWA, on Sunday and Monday.\n\nAs far as thunderstorm chances go late this week, cool low \nlevel air wrapped up in the low will result in weak lapse rates \nthrough the column and little to no instability for noteworthy \nthunder coverage. Nonetheless, a few embedded storms will \ncertainly be possible here and there. If the low can take a \nfarther north track, then chances would increase for embedded, \nnon-severe storms for areas in the low's warm sector, but such a\ntrack is an outlier among medium-range guidance. Despite the \nlack of thunder, deep layer moisture and dynamic forcing with \nthe stacked low could result in prolonged periods of soaking \nrainfall; all available deterministic camps resolve a widespread\n1-2\"+ of QPF through Sunday morning.\n\nAfternoon temperatures are forecast in the upper 70s and lower \n80s this weekend. A gradual warming trend is expected next week \nwith highs favored to climb back toward 90 by the end of the \nweek.\n\nDoom",
